= Incentive =

== Overview ==
'''Incentive''' is a feature of an environment, rule, or consequence that changes the relative attractiveness of actions to an agent.

Incentives may be monetary or non-monetary, positive or negative, explicit or implicit, immediate or delayed. They operate through the preferences, constraints, expectations, and information of the actor.

The effect of an incentive is empirical and context-dependent. A nominal reward does not guarantee more of the targeted behavior, and poorly designed incentives can produce substitution, gaming, crowding effects, or attention to measurable proxies.

In strategic systems, incentives interact with information and the behavior of others. Mechanism design and principal–agent theory formalize important subsets of this problem.

== Boundaries ==
* incentive ≠ reward only
* stated incentive ≠ actual behavioral effect
* incentive alignment ≠ identical interests

